	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105022889495?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Caesarea Maritima, founded in the first century bce by Herod the Great, was the capital of the Greco-Roman province of Palestine largely due to its major port, which insured Caesarea’s economic importance to the region. The aim of this study is to better characterize the agricultural economy in this period and the types of land-use practices employed in the region using archaeobotanical remains. Here we present an analysis of 95 sediment samples, totalling 908.5 L of sediment that were recovered and processed for botanical remains from a variety of contexts dating to the Byzantine period. The samples contained 15,198 botanical specimens representing 92 plant taxa and were recovered from domestic deposits, commercial storage, refuse, fill and destruction layers. The most common species are typical agricultural crops, such as wheat, barley, olive, grape, fig, stone pine, lentil and bitter vetch, which confirms their economic importance. However, it is difficult to determine whether taxa were being grown locally or imported. For example, the ratio of almost 2:1 grain to chaff, as well as high frequencies of agricultural weeds support both local production and importation of cereal crops at Caesarea, as both were likely needed to support the city’s population and for export to the wider region. Other data we examine are the remains of tree and vine crops, weeds, and agricultural evidence presented in ancient literary sources that add to our understanding of what agricultural commodities were being grown, consumed and exported from Caesarea and how the land surrounding the site was being used to support the economy. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2025.},
