	abstract = {International trade in agricultural commodities is shaped by environmental and geopolitical regulations that determine access to international markets. This study examines the factors influencing market access for Colombian palm oil exports between 2011 and 2023, a period marked by sectoral consolidation. Employing a quantitative analytical methodology, the research utilizes a gravity model with Poisson Pseudo-Maximum Likelihood (PPML) estimation to analyze international trade flows. The dataset features a country-year panel covering 14 importing markets and 182 observations. Results from two complementary econometric specifications show that productive capacity, geographic proximity, and trade agreements still partially explain trade flows. However, regulatory frameworks and new sustainability standards are increasingly setting the terms for global market access. The findings point to a shift from traditional gravity model perspectives, focused on economic, geographic, and trade factors, toward approaches influenced by evolving regulatory regimes. © 2026, Universidad del Zulia. All rights reserved.},
