	doi = {10.1016/j.ecoenv.2026.120334},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046761767?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Zearalenone (ZEN) and prochloraz (PCZ) are widespread environmental pollutants that often co-exist in agricultural commodities, raising concerns for human health. Despite this, the combined toxic effects of ZEN and PCZ, particularly across generations, remain largely unexplored. In this study, we comprehensively assessed the co-toxicity of ZEN and PCZ in adult female zebrafish (F₀ generation) and examined the repercussions on their non-exposed progeny (F₁ generation). Adult female fish were exposed for 60 days to sublethal experimental concentrations of ZEN, PCZ, or their combination (COT) under controlled experimental conditions. The results indicated a marked synergistic acute toxicity in the COT group, with a synergistic ratio of 3.05. In the F₀ generation, COT exposure induced severe intestinal damage, including villus atrophy, reduced diamine oxidase activity, and heightened intestinal permeability, along with increased D-lactate and lipopolysaccharide levels. Additionally, combined exposure disrupted gut microbial composition and triggered hepatotoxicity, evidenced by extensive hepatocyte degeneration and elevated ALT/AST activities. Reproductive organs were also markedly affected, with the COT group showing impaired ovarian function, disrupted steroidogenesis, and hormonal imbalance. Importantly, these parental exposures exerted notable intergenerational effects: F₁ larvae displayed developmental anomalies such as spinal curvature and abdominal edema, accompanied by altered expression of steroidogenesis-related genes and disturbed oxidative stress balance. Overall, these findings revealed that simultaneous exposure to ZEN and PCZ generated strong synergistic toxicity through joint impairment of the gut-liver axis and reproductive endocrine system, highlighting ecological hazards that surpassed predictions based on individual compounds. This study further emphasized the necessity of incorporating intergenerational outcomes into environmental risk assessments of chemical mixtures. Copyright © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	note = {All Open Access; Gold Open Access}
}

